## STEP 8 — Behavior Implementation

### Why we create it
Supplies the actual ABAP logic for every determination, validation, and action declared in Step 7. This is where the Utility Class and Exception Class get wired together — the only place custom business logic executes in this whole BO.

### Where it is used
Invoked automatically by the RAP runtime at the exact lifecycle points named in the BDEF (`on modify`, `on save`, action call) — you never call these methods directly.

### How RAP uses it
The generated local class `lhc_SampleRequest` inherits from `cl_abap_behavior_handler`. RAP instantiates it per request, calls the matching method with a `keys` table (or `keys FOR ACTION ...`), and reads back `result`/`mapped`/`failed`/`reported` after each call.

### Common mistakes
- Calling `MODIFY ENTITIES` without `IN LOCAL MODE` — required within Managed RAP handler methods operating on the same BO.
- Forgetting to populate `reported-samplerequest` even for successful instances (needed so the framework doesn't treat the message channel as stale) — I do this only where relevant (validations); actions only report on failure.
- Re-implementing the monthly-cap SELECT inline instead of calling `zcl_sdt_sample_util=>is_monthly_cap_hit` — direct rule violation of "never duplicate logic."
- Not restricting `Approve`/`Reject`/`Validate` to only fire from the correct source status — without `get_instance_features`, the UI would show all three buttons active regardless of state, and users could double-approve.

### Activation order
Last object dependent on Steps 1–7 — must activate **after all of them**, and specifically after the BDEF quick-fix has generated the class stub (Step 7's last testing step).

